nodecode-watchline is a rewrite of qordinate-ai's qordinate-ai/watchline-hermes-plugin:

  https://github.com/qordinate-ai/watchline-hermes-plugin
  commit d3d2424336af16cae94e4bbcb47e6edd07a88062
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Watchline (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin registered a Hermes gateway platform that polled a
Watchline pull channel (`POST /v1/deliveries.pending`), formatted each matched
event as the inbound message its harness received, and acknowledged the ids
(`POST /v1/deliveries.ack`) only after the harness took them. All of that is
here, over the same two routes with the same bearer key and the same request
shape: a worker polls every `watchline.poll_interval_seconds`, puts each
delivery into the `watchline` session through the in-process ingress with one
command id per delivery (so a re-poll admits a duplicate rather than starting a
second turn), and acknowledges the pass in one call. The message the event
becomes is the original's `format_delivery` line for line -- the user's intent,
"use this event as fresh context", the event as JSON, or for a watch that needs
the operator, the action and its connection links -- under the platform hint
the original carried, that the event is not a message from a person. Its
`preview-delivery` command survives as `/watchline preview`, its `status` as
`/watchline status`, and the config it wrote into the harness (platform entry
plus MCP server, `configure` and `install-mcp`) became the cell's `watchline`
section, which `/settings` writes. One tool is new: `watchline_pending`, the
pass read without acknowledging anything, because a model that can see what is
waiting is worth more than one that only learns of it after the fact.

Left out: the MCP server entry the original installed into Hermes config
(Watchline's hosted MCP tools are the harness's own business, and a Nodecode
agent reaches HTTP APIs directly), the Hermes `config.yaml` writing and its
`configure`/`install-mcp` commands (a cell's config is its declared section),
and the plugin manifest's `WATCHLINE_*` variables other than the key and
channel, which are section members here.
